mat":{"pdf":{"documentclass":"article","papersize":"letter","geometry":["margin=1in"],"fontsize":"10pt","number-sections":true,"colorlinks":true},"html":{"theme":"cosmo","toc":true,"number-sections":true}},"engines":[{"path":"/Users/aron/.local/share/extension-subtrees/julia-engine/_extensions/julia-engine/julia-engine.js"}],"quarto-internal":{"subarticles":[]},"notebook-preview-options":{"back":true},"theme":"cosmo","title-block-style":"manuscript","lightbox":"auto","documentclass":"article","papersize":"letter","geometry":["margin=1in"],"fontsize":"10pt","colorlinks":true,"title":"LLM Linguistic Evolution: Language and Cooperation in LLM Dyads","author":[{"name":"Anonymous Author(s)","affiliations":[{"id":"aff1","name":"Anonymous Institution"}]}],"date":"last-modified","bibliography":["references.json"],"csl":"neurips.csl","google-scholar":false,"abstract":"Can natural-language exchange between LLM agents become behaviourally load-bearing, or does it merely accompany decisions already fixed by model and prompt? We introduce a controlled dyadic paradigm that pairs a role-swapping repeated Trust Game with an optional iterated myth-writing task. Across GPT-5-Nano and Claude Sonnet 4.5, we vary task order, action-channel noise, and neutral role framing, and measure both game behaviour and the evolving text. The preliminary pattern is cautionary: model identity and payoff-channel perturbations dominate mean cooperation, while myth-writing produces small, model-dependent behavioural deltas rather than a robust cooperation lift. Its clearest signal is instead in strategy consolidation and linguistic convergence within dyads. These results position the paradigm as a concept-and-feasibility probe for studying cultural dynamics in LLM agents, and identify the controls needed for a stronger later submission.","clear-hidden-classes":"none","remove-hidden":"all","unroll-markdown-cells":true},"extensions":{"book":{"selfContainedOutput":true}}}}